{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent negative bias: forecasts are generally below realized prices, especially during sustained uptrends. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +1.0 to +2.5 EUR) when the last 5–10 days show rising prices / higher highs.",
    "Trend lag / underreaction: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts upward (misses step-ups). If spot breaks above the prior 10–15d range, lift the entire 1–20d forecast path upward and steepen the near-term slope.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: underprediction is largest in the front/mid horizon (~days 5–20). Use a hump-shaped correction (bigger uplift mid-horizon, smaller at day 1–3 and day 25–30).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot risk: the model sometimes projects excessive continuation into the far tail (e.g., very high day 25–30 levels) while realized prices flatten or reverse. Cap far-horizon upside by blending day 20–30 toward a mean-reverting anchor (e.g., recent 10–20d average).",
    "Reversal miss after peaks: when realized prices roll over, the model can stay too high or mis-time the drop. If the last 3–5 days show lower highs / downside momentum, dampen the day 10–30 trajectory and pull it toward the recent mean faster."
  ]
}
